# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Downgraded from high to medium because the impact is availability-only (process crash) with no confidentiality/integrity compromise, and exploitation depends on a specific aarch64 hardware feature mismatch plus a pure-rust build. Default binding is localhost, and no deployment manifests indicate public exposure.
## Likelihood
high - Requires a pure-rust aarch64 build and a CPU with CRC+AES but no PMULL; if present, a single checksum-bearing request can trigger the crash. Deployment exposure is configuration-dependent. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - If the PMULL backend is selected on a CPU lacking PMULL, vmull_p64 can SIGILL and terminate the server process, causing availability loss for the instance.
## Assumptions
- argmin-s3 is built with the pure-rust CRC feature on aarch64 (ISA-L not available on that target).
- The deployment runs on an aarch64 CPU that reports CRC+AES but lacks PMULL.
- An attacker can send requests that cause CRC32/CRC32C computation (checksum headers or trailers) and can reach the HTTP service (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR not localhost-only).
- argmin-s3 compiled with pure-rust CRC on aarch64
- CPU has CRC+AES but not PMULL
- attacker can issue requests that trigger CRC32/CRC32C validation
- service reachable on its configured listen address
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4
## Path evidence
- `crates/checksum/src/crc32.rs:170-207` - aarch64 backend selection uses has_crc_pmull_aarch64(), which only checks CRC+AES (no PMULL).
- `crates/checksum/src/crc32.rs:482-499` - aarch64 PMULL path uses vmull_p64 under target_feature("crc,neon,aes") without PMULL gating.
- `crates/checksum/src/crc32c.rs:202-206` - CRC32C aarch64 PMULL backend selection checks CRC+AES only.
- `crates/checksum/src/crc32c.rs:464-482` - CRC32C aarch64 PMULL implementation uses vmull_p64 under target_feature without PMULL.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:47-114` - Streaming uploads create CRC32/CRC32C hashers based on request trailer headers.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4639-4658` - Checksum headers trigger CRC32/CRC32C computation over request bodies.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:24-62` - HTTP service listen address defaults to 127.0.0.1:9000; exposure depends on configuration.
## Narrative
The aarch64 pure-rust CRC32/CRC32C backend is selected when has_crc_pmull_aarch64() returns true, but that check only verifies "crc" and "aes" and omits "pmull". The aarch64 fast path uses vmull_p64 under #[target_feature(enable = "crc,neon,aes")], so on hardware that has AES+CRC but no PMULL, the server can execute an illegal instruction and crash. The HTTP layer computes CRC32/CRC32C over attacker-controlled request bodies when checksum headers/trailers are present, making this reachable from normal S3 request flows. Default binding is localhost and a PoC was described but not executed, so exposure depends on deployment and hardware specifics.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication for standard S3 operations
- Checksum header/trailer validation
- Configurable max connections/in-flight limits
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests or ingress configs to confirm public exposure.
- No runtime PoC executed in this environment; hardware feature mismatch not verified here.
- Build feature selection for production aarch64 targets not confirmed beyond Cargo feature definitions.
